{
  "assessment": "The paper provides a quantitative model for the static rolling friction of liquid marbles, with explicit equations, fitted parameters, and experimental data across multiple regimes. It supports at least one numeric control (marble volume, surface fraction), numeric readouts (departure angle, static friction, curvature difference, raft yielding force), and quantitative mechanisms (energy balance model, logistic f(phi)). Case authoring is ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Yui Takai, Static Friction of Liquid Marbles, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/njnj-yhc6",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/njnj-yhc6",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2506.20116v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper investigates the static rolling friction of liquid marbles (water droplets coated with lycopodium particles) on tilted substrates. It develops a model based on shear yielding of the granular shell, validates it against departure-angle measurements for varying marble volume and particle surface fraction, and extends the logistic form of the intergrain force function to two-dimensional granular rafts.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "rolling_friction_energy_balance",
      "statement": "The onset of rolling of a liquid marble is governed by an energy balance: the gravitational energy gain during rolling must exceed the shear dissipation in the granular shell. The shell is modeled as a purely plastic, quasi-static granular raft with yield stress q_y = mu p(phi), where mu is a macroscopic friction coefficient and p(phi) is the mean normal stress. The effective surface tension is gamma_eff = gamma - p(phi) d = gamma(1 - f(phi)), introducing the dimensionless function f(phi).",
      "equations": [
        "alpha ≈ (S a^2 / Omega) (delta_epsilon / delta_x) mu f(phi)/(1 - f(phi))",
        "alpha ≈ (1/2)(1 + sqrt(8 pi a^3 / Omega)) mu f(phi)/(1 - f(phi))",
        "alpha ≈ (6 pi^2)^{1/3} (a^3 / Omega)^{2/3} mu f(phi)/(1 - f(phi))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "alpha",
        "S",
        "a",
        "Omega",
        "delta_epsilon",
        "delta_x",
        "mu",
        "f(phi)",
        "phi",
        "gamma_eff",
        "gamma",
        "p(phi)",
        "d"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "mu_large_marbles",
          "value": 0.053,
          "uncertainty": "±0.003 (standard error, source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Macroscopic friction coefficient fitted to large-marble departure-angle data",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "Fitting Eq. (model_large) in the large-marble regime with f(phi_o)=0.26 and a(phi_o)=2.3 mm gives mu=0.053 ± 0.003."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "f_phi_o",
          "value": 0.26,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Dimensionless intergrain force function at initial surface fraction phi_o",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "Fitting Eq. (model_large) with f(phi_o)=0.26."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "a_phi_o",
          "value": 0.0023,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "m",
          "role": "Capillary length at phi_o, corresponding to gamma_eff=53 mN/m",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "a(phi_o) ≈ 2.3 mm, i.e. gamma_eff(phi_o) ≈ 53 mN/m."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "small_marble_prefactor",
          "value": 0.69,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Empirical prefactor adjusting the small-marble model to data",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 122,
              "line_end": 122,
              "support": "Eq. (model_small) predicts the small-Omega data with a prefactor of 0.69."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 86,
          "line_end": 89,
          "support": "Departure angle determined from energy argument: gravitational energy gain exceeds shear dissipation in granular shell; shell modeled as purely plastic with yield stress q_y."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 90,
          "line_end": 91,
          "support": "Mohr-Coulomb criterion q_y = mu p(phi); effective surface tension gamma_eff = gamma - p(phi) d."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 93,
          "line_end": 95,
          "support": "Pressure term rewritten as p(phi)=gamma f(phi)/d; gamma_eff = gamma(1-f(phi))."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 96,
          "line_end": 101,
          "support": "Energy balance yields Eq. (model_general): alpha ≈ (S a^2/Omega)(delta_epsilon/delta_x) mu f/(1-f)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 104,
          "line_end": 107,
          "support": "Large-marble geometry: flattened to height 2a, cylinder approximation, S ≈ pi l^2/2 + 2 pi l a, delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/(2a)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 109,
          "line_end": 113,
          "support": "Combining geometry gives Eq. (model_large): alpha ≈ (1/2)(1+sqrt(8 pi a^3/Omega)) mu f/(1-f)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 117,
          "line_end": 121,
          "support": "Small-marble geometry: S ≈ 2 pi R l, delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/l, giving Eq. (model_small): alpha ≈ (6 pi^2)^{1/3} (a^3/Omega)^{2/3} mu f/(1-f)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "logistic_f_phi",
      "statement": "The dimensionless intergrain force function f(phi) follows a logistic function of the particle surface fraction phi, with inflection point at phi_c and steepness beta. This form is derived from the large-marble model in the asymptotic limit and confirmed by direct measurements on liquid marbles and granular rafts.",
      "equations": [
        "f(phi) = 1 / (1 + exp(-beta (phi - phi_c)))",
        "phi_c = phi_o - (1/beta) ln( gamma / gamma_eff(phi_o) - 1 )"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "f(phi)",
        "phi",
        "beta",
        "phi_c",
        "phi_o",
        "gamma",
        "gamma_eff"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "beta_marbles",
          "value": 15.4,
          "uncertainty": "±1.7 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Steepness of logistic f(phi) for liquid marbles",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 155,
              "line_end": 155,
              "support": "Data collapse onto alpha/alpha_o = exp[beta(phi-phi_o)] with beta = 15.4 ± 1.7."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 556,
              "line_end": 556,
              "support": "Table: lycopodium marbles beta = 15.4 ± 1.7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "phi_c_marbles",
          "value": 0.9,
          "uncertainty": "±0.01 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Inflection point of logistic f(phi) for liquid marbles",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 164,
              "line_end": 164,
              "support": "phi_c ≈ 0.90 ± 0.01."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 556,
              "line_end": 556,
              "support": "Table: lycopodium marbles phi_c = 90 ± 1%."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 159,
          "line_end": 164,
          "support": "From experimental result and Eq. (model_large) in asymptotic limit, f(phi) = 1/(1+exp(-beta(phi-phi_c))) with phi_c = phi_o - (1/beta) ln(gamma/gamma_eff(phi_o)-1), phi_c ≈ 0.90 ± 0.01."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 166,
          "line_end": 166,
          "support": "Fig. F4a shows f(phi) estimated from departure angles using Eq. (model_large) agrees with Eq. (f_th)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 189,
          "line_end": 190,
          "support": "Raft measurements also follow logistic; all data collapse onto logistic prediction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "raft_yielding_force",
      "statement": "In two-dimensional granular rafts, the force required to yield the raft with a dragged fiber scales with the intergrain force function f(phi). The normalized yielding force F_y/F_y_max follows the same logistic function as f(phi) for liquid marbles.",
      "equations": [
        "F_y = k delta",
        "F_y(phi) = F_max / (1 + exp(-beta (phi - phi_c)))",
        "f(phi) = F_y(phi) / F_y_max"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "F_y",
        "k",
        "delta",
        "phi",
        "beta",
        "phi_c",
        "F_max",
        "f(phi)"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "k_fiber_range_min",
          "value": 0.01,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "N/m",
          "role": "Minimum effective spring constant of the fiber",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 177,
              "line_end": 177,
              "support": "k = 10-60 mN/m for long to short fibers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "k_fiber_range_max",
          "value": 0.06,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "N/m",
          "role": "Maximum effective spring constant of the fiber",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 177,
              "line_end": 177,
              "support": "k = 10-60 mN/m for long to short fibers."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 177,
          "line_end": 178,
          "support": "Fiber deflection delta gives yielding force F_y = k delta; expected F_y ~ q_y ~ f(phi)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 179,
          "line_end": 179,
          "support": "Normalize F_y by asymptotic value F_y_max to probe f(phi)=F_y/F_y_max."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 189,
          "line_end": 190,
          "support": "Raft measurements follow logistic; all data collapse onto logistic prediction."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
